\section{Why this is a rescale and not a model}{

There is no physical mapping from crown bulk density to a probability of
contributing to spread, and this function does not invent one. It puts the
metric on the scale the graded path expects, in the way you choose, and
records the choice. The result is an ordering of cells by measured fuel, not
an estimate of anything.

That is why the method matters and why it is recorded: \code{"minmax"} says a cell
at half the maximum load contributes half, \code{"percentile_rank"} says a cell in
the median contributes half. Those are different claims and the data does not
choose between them.
}

\section{What \code{NA} means here, and why it is not zero}{

LiDAR HD has not flown everywhere, and where it has not, the metric is \code{NA}.
\code{NA} is \strong{not} "no fuel" — it is "not measured", and the difference decides
whether the resulting exposure map is a measurement or a fiction.

This function leaves \code{NA} as \code{NA}. \code{\link[=fev_exposure]{fev_exposure()}} with its default
\code{na_rm = FALSE} then returns \code{NA} for any cell whose neighbourhood is not
fully measured, which looks like a hole and is one. Filling it would take a
campaign covering 2 km² of the Maures and quietly extend its authority over
the other 300.

\code{fill} exists for the case where you have a defensible value for the
unmeasured ground — a class median from \code{\link[=fev_fuel_availability]{fev_fuel_availability()}}, say — and
it writes into the record that you supplied it.
}

